"We do not see it in the place where it was kept," they reported. The King ordered the detention of those living within the palace, saying, "Search for the jewel and return it." Seeing those who were detained, the Elder Ānanda explained a certain stratagem to the jewel-keepers. They reported this to the King, and the King said, "The Elder is wise; do as the Elder says."

The jewel-keepers placed a large water jar in the palace courtyard, surrounded it with a curtain, and told the people, "Wrap yourselves in a cloak, come here, and reach your hands into the jar." The thief of the jewel thought, "It is impossible to either sell or use this royal treasure." He went home, placed the jewel in his armpit, came back wrapped in his cloak, dropped it into the water jar, and departed. 

When the crowd had dispersed, the King's men reached their hands into the jar, found the jewel, took it out, and presented it to the King. "The jewel has been found by the method shown by the Elder Ānanda!" the people cried out in a great commotion. The monks reported this matter to the Blessed One.

The Teacher said, "Monks, it is not wonderful that Ānanda has now recovered a jewel that had reached the hands of men; in the past, wise ones, relying on their own wisdom, recovered treasure that had reached the hands of even animals born with rootless rebirth-consciousness (ahetuka-paṭisandhi) and gave it to the King." Having said this, he told the Mahāsāra Jātaka:

"In times of peril, they desire a hero;
In counsel, one who is composed;
In food and drink, a dear one;
But when a matter of importance arises, they desire a wise person."

"Na taṃ daḷhaṃ" means that the wise do not call that bond a firm or strong bond. "Yadāyasaṃ" means a bond made of iron. "Sārattarattā" means being intensely lustful, or being attached by perceiving, "This is the essence." "Apekkhā" means attachment or longing. "Āhū" means they say. "Ohārinaṃ" means having the nature of dragging one down to the four lower realms. "Sithilaṃ" means it is not like an iron bond that restricts one's posture; for those bound by this [attachment] can still go to other places. "Duppamuñcaṃ" means it is impossible to be released by any means other than supramundane knowledge (lokuttara-ñāṇa). The tenth sutta is finished.

